WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:09.481
A payment service builds its objects by hand in main, each one after the objects its constructor needs.

2
00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:12.871
A dependency injection container does that for you.

3
00:00:12.871 --> 00:00:14.693
Here is the one you'll build.

4
00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:20.587
At start-up it finds the components, orders them, and builds each singleton once.

5
00:00:20.587 --> 00:00:22.952
Timed and Retry methods get a proxy.

6
00:00:22.952 --> 00:00:27.681
A generator can write the same wiring at build time, for a faster start.

7
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.825
Lesson 1, in Java, wires classes by reflection.

8
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.447
The scanner loads each class without initializing it, so no static initializer runs.

9
00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:42.281
It never guesses a constructor, and it skips the synthetic ones Kotlin adds.

10
00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:46.868
A depth-first walk orders the graph, and names any cycle at start-up.

11
00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:53.732
Each singleton gets a cell with double-checked locking: one instance, even when eight threads ask at once.

12
00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:57.289
Lesson 2, in Kotlin, adds Timed and Retry.

13
00:00:57.467 --> 00:01:01.612
Interceptors run around each call, with Timed outside Retry.

14
00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:06.737
A component with an interface gets a JDK proxy around its target.

15
00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:10.052
A class without one gets a Byte Buddy subclass.

16
00:01:10.052 --> 00:01:16.291
A subclass can't override what Kotlin leaves final, so the container refuses that at start-up.

17
00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:20.228
Lesson 3, in Java, measures it and makes it start fast.

18
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:27.980
Per call, JMH counts 208 bytes for a JDK proxy, and 160 for a subclass.

19
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.239
At start-up, the app loads 2,207 classes.

20
00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:36.932
A class histogram and a heap dump show what the container keeps alive.

21
00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:43.861
Then a generator runs the same graph at build time, and writes a holder class per singleton.

22
00:01:43.861 --> 00:01:46.128
The start drops to 991 classes.

23
00:01:46.333 --> 00:01:47.444
It writes Java.

24
00:01:47.444 --> 00:01:50.997
In Kotlin, an object is the same kind of holder.

25
00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:55.412
AppCDS and the AOT cache then archive what is left.

26
00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:59.510
One rule holds throughout: never guess, never skip in silence.

27
00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:06.195
What the classes alone can tell, the container checks before the first call, at start-up, or in the build.
